WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:04.920
Thank you.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.680
So I. I was thinking, earlier.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.670
Like, what idiot scheduled
this talk at this time?

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:11.215
And, like.

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:12.590
Because immediately
afterwards, I

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.850
have to go and introduce
Joe for the keynote.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.170
And of course, the idiot was me.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.960
So today I want
to give you a bit

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:28.100
of a first look at this new
OOP system for R called R7.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.530
And obviously like
I'm giving this talk,

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.000
but I didn't do all
the work for this talk.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.200
This is a joint effort from
the R Consortium working group

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.680
on object oriented programming.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.860
So I'll talk a little
bit about that later

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.260
because I think that's a really
important part of this project.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.100
This is not just like
some idea that I've had,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.520
it's a team effort from a number
of very important stakeholders

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.100
in the R community.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.910
But to begin, I wanted to
kind of talk about like,

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.760
why do we need OOP?

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:03.780
And I want to do that by talking
about this bizarro function.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.530
And the goal of this bizarro
function is to take an R object

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.060
and turn it upside down somehow.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.840
So for example, if we've
got a numeric vector,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.020
maybe we'll flip the sign.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.940
Or if we've got a logical
vector, maybe I'll turn

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.030
true to falses and
falses to trues.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.980
Or if I've got a
character vector,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.770
I'll flip the letters in each
of the strings in that vector.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.930
Or if I've got a factor,
I'll flip the levels.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:36.260
And if there's anything
else, I'll just throw an error.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.610
So what's wrong
with this function?

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.520
Well, there's nothing really
wrong with this function

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.610
because it's so simple.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.650
Like you can already
see it on one slide.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:50.020
But there are some problems with
this kind of general approach.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.280
And the first one is like as
we handle more and more types

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.190
of data, this function is
going to get bigger and bigger

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.900
and bigger, and you're
going to have all

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.270
of that code in a single file.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.120
And if you think about
functions in base R

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.340
that have to have
different behavior

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.910
for different types of things.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.850
This is going to become
really problematic

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.950
and I think the
function in base R

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.770
that does the most different
things for the most different

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.080
types of objects is print.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.910
And if you were to write print
with this kind of if-else style,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.280
like this is what it
looks like just for the,

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.890
the types of objects in base R

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.040
that begin with a letter
A. Like that's already not

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:31.840
enough to fit on a
slide and I haven't even

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.590
included the implementations.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.423
So you can imagine
as this gets, as you

00:02:35.423 --> 00:02:37.090
deal with more and
more types of things,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.080
it's getting bigger and
bigger and bigger.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.990
And the other possibly
more important problem

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.600
is that there's
only one person who

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.840
can add new types of
behavior to that function,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.900
that they can teach that function
to handle new types of object,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.840
and that's the original author.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.240
And again, you can
imagine for R core,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:01.470
like if every time someone
added a new type of thing,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:06.690
a new class, that some code in
R itself would have to change,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.750
that's obviously going to
be a huge, huge hassle.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:16.250
So that's really the inspiration
for OO programming in R.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.210
But there are already two
types of OO systems in R.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.440
So why do we have S4?

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.450
Well, how does R7 fit in?

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.640
Well, first of all, we
have S3 or mama bear.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.380
Now S3 was it has its
name because it was the OOP

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.100
system developed for the third
version of the S programming

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:37.900
language.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.940
It really is the
simplest OOP system

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.740
that could possibly work.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.690
It works basically
mostly on conventions.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.410
It doesn't have a lot of
structure and it works great,

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.390
but it's very, very simple.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.150
And then a few years later, as
kind of a reaction to history,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.940
along came Papa Bear, S4.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.590
And S4 is very
strict, very rigorous.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.960
It's a lot you get a
lot more guarantees,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.720
but it's also a lot more
complicated and a lot more

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:07.740
work.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.730
So S4, so-called because it was
designed for the fourth version

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.050
of the S programming language.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.170
And so now we've been working
on this tool called R7,

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:19.700
where we want to be--

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.200
We want to try and
be like baby bear.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.570
We want to be like just right.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.780
We want to strike the right
balance between complexity

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.010
and power.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.260
And we're calling it
R7 because it's

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:36.050
hopefully like 3 plus 4, like
the best parts of S3 and S4

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.100
and it's designed for
R, not for like backward

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.470
compatibility with S.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.090
So what would it
look like if we were

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.880
going to implement this
bizarro function in R7?

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.510
Well, the first thing we have
to do is load the R7 package,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.310
and this is kind of
probably temporary.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.430
So currently R7 is just
a package on GitHub.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.480
Hopefully in a few months
it'll become a package on CRAN,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.550
but the long term goal is
to get it into R itself.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:06.130
So this doesn't this is not
an additional dependency.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.980
This is something
that's built into R.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.820
But for now, we're going
to load that package.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.780
And then we're going to
create a generic, and a generic

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.620
as a special type of function
whose behavior depends

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:22.790
on the type of one or
more other functions.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.690
So here we're defining
a new generic.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.410
And crazily enough, we're
going to use a function called

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.050
new generic to do
that, and we're

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.340
going to give that function
the name of the generic.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:33.420
So bizaro.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.410
And we're also going to store
that result in a variable

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.862
called bizarro, that is
going to be our generic.

00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:40.820
So it's a little bit
unfortunate that we've got

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.060
to have this repetition here.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.030
We're saying bizarro twice.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.640
We considered a number of
ways to work around this

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.160
and decided that this was
the least worst solution

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.090
was this a little
bit of repetition.

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:55.280
There's one other
argument here, and that

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.560
is the name of the
argument that's

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.310
going to be used for dispatch.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.930
Dispatch is the process of
finding the implementation,

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.280
given the type of the argument.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.700
So this generic, like,
doesn't really do anything.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.220
There's no code here, right?

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.380
There's no implementation.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:15.280
But it kind of defines the
shape of what this function is

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.540
going to look like.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:19.030
And if we want to add
implementation, what we

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.480
have to do is define methods.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:24.480
So if we go back to that bizarro
function, look at that first

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.340
if block if is numeric x what
we're going to do now instead

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.920
of an if block is we're
going to define a method.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.620
And we're going to define
that method with the method

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.360
function.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:39.150
The first argument of the
method is the generic,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.670
and the second
argument to the generic

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.670
is the type of object we want
to provide an implementation

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.840
or a method for.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.550
And so here we're going
to use a built-in class.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:51.030
This is something a numeric
vectors of something that come

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.980
built into R something
that R7

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.520
is going to provide for you.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.820
And then we provide the
implementation, which

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.680
is exactly the same as before.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.090
So the implementation
has not changed.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:03.420
But the way we get to
that implementation,

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.430
instead of having a bunch
of if-else statements,

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.067
we're going to use this,
this method lookup.

00:07:08.067 --> 00:07:09.900
And so if we work through
the other methods,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.280
the logical exactly
the same thing.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.970
Now instead of testing,
if it is dot  logical,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:19.080
we say we're defining a method
for the bizarro generic

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.360
with logical class.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.820
Same thing for
character and so on.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.460
And once we've done
that, the generic

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.170
is going to work
exactly the same way

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.260
as our original function.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.070
Nothing's going to differ there,
except that when we call it

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.755
with some type of object we
haven't provided a method for,

00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:37.630
we're going to get a
slightly different error

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:41.398
message because we're
going to say the R7 R7

00:07:41.398 --> 00:07:42.940
system is going to
say, well, I can't

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:45.020
find a method for an object

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.620
that's a function.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.350
If we print that
generic, we can see

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:54.910
that this is an R7 generic
that has a bunch of methods.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:57.370
And if you're paying
attention, you

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.130
might notice that there
are four methods there.

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.620
But I only just
defined three methods.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:05.110
And that's because
the numeric class

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:08.650
is a little bit of a fiction.
In R there isn't really

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.720
such a thing as
a numeric vector.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.050
A numeric vector is either
actually an integer vector

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:15.410
or a double vector.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.790
And that's what you
can see in this class.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.470
Now, you've probably
already you've

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.870
probably seen a similar
thing if you've ever

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.870
printed an S3
generic. But this is what

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.060
it S3 generic looks like.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.950
So if you've ever been
hunting for the source

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:33.130
code for a function and you
see this weird use method

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.970
call, that tells you you're
dealing with an S3 generic.

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:39.400
And so one of the things we've
tried to do with R7

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:40.905
is to be a little
bit more helpful.

00:08:40.905 --> 00:08:43.030
When you look at a generic,
it's going to tell you,

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:45.520
hey, this is a generic
and these are the methods

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.080
that I know about.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.310
And those four methods there
which look like function calls,

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.060
those are actually
function calls.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.593
And if you copy and paste
that code into your console,

00:08:55.593 --> 00:08:57.885
you'll see the implementation
of each of those methods.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.020
So there are two parts to
object oriented programming

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.950
systems in R. Generics-

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:10.470
generic functions which define
behavior, and classes, which

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:11.560
define data.

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:12.827
So I've shown you generics.

00:09:12.827 --> 00:09:14.910
And now I want to show you
a little bit about what

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:18.320
classes in R7 look like.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.660
So I'm going to
create a new class.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.110
Surprise, surprise.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:25.100
I'm going to use a function
called new class to do that.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.210
I'm going to call
it a range class.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:30.170
Again, I'm going to assign
the result of that function

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.870
to an object with the
same name as the class,

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:35.570
and that is going to produce
what we call the constructor

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:36.420
function.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.180
This is both kind
of a definition

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.190
of the class and
the thing you're

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:45.440
going to use to create instances
or objects of that class.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.010
This class has some properties.
So properties are the data

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:50.770
that an object possesses.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.910
If you've used S3 before,
this is the same as attributes.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.810
If you've used S4,
these are the same as slots.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:02.450
So these are the, the, the data
that every object of this range

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.210
class is going to
possess, every one is going

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.030
to have a start property
and an end property

00:10:08.030 --> 00:10:11.630
that are both numeric vectors.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.600
So now that we've created that
object, that class object,

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.480
that class constructor, we
can create range objects

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.280
just by calling that function.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:20.390
We can print it.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.480
We get some information
about the data that's in it,

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:25.610
and we get this
hint that maybe we

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:28.040
can access the values
inside that object

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:29.840
with the at symbol.

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:31.910
So if you've ever
used S4 before,

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.590
that's the way you
extract data out

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:39.270
of objects in the S4 with
that and R7 takes that.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.680
So you can get values,
you can set values.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.360
But R7 has kind of got
some built in safety rails

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.420
so that if you try
and assign a character

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:52.977
vector to that x property that
we said had to be a numeric,

00:10:52.977 --> 00:10:54.185
you're going to get an error.

00:10:57.340 --> 00:11:00.580
But that doesn't protect you
against every possible error

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:03.335
or every possible way you
might create an invalid object.

00:11:03.335 --> 00:11:04.960
Because you might
say, well, actually I

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.570
want to have a range object
where the start is always

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:10.900
less than the end, and there's
currently nothing stopping me

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.660
from creating invalid ranges
just by using a larger

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.750
start than an end.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.760
You can resolve that
in R7 by providing

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.450
something called a validator.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:23.370
A validator is just a
function that takes an object

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:26.100
and either says,
all as well, or it

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.880
returns a message telling you
what is wrong with that object.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.820
And so once you
supply a validator,

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.880
then that is going to
ensure that every instance

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.970
of that object has to be valid.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.620
It's very well,
it's still it's R,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.720
it's still possible to
create an invalid object,

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.090
but you've got to jump
through some hoops to do that.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.450
And this is a really valuable,
really valuable property

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:52.980
because this means that if you
have a range object, you know

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:55.600
that start is always
going to be less than end.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.480
You know that these
conditions are true,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.940
which is really useful.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.860
Another really cool
thing you can do

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:06.270
is you can make properties
that aren't actually data

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.110
that are computed on demand.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:11.830
They behave exactly as if there
was some data behind them.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.250
But you can do
basically anything you

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:15.850
want inside these properties.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:19.530
So these work a little like

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.620
active bindings if
you've used those before.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.398
But this is a really important
aspect of R7,

00:12:25.398 --> 00:12:27.440
which which I'll come back
to a little bit later.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:35.690
So that that's kind of
R7 in a nutshell.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.870
But how does it kind of compare
to S3 and S4?

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.380
Well, I think if we're going
to compare OO systems,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.530
it's good to think about what's
the complexity, how hard it

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:46.620
is to use,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:48.590
and what's the payoff.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:52.170
And S3 has a very,
very low complexity.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:56.610
It's very, very simple, and
the payoff is relatively low.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.970
S4 the payoff is
much, much higher,

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:03.480
but the complexity is
also much, much higher.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:07.590
And so our goal with R7 is
to create something that's

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:11.220
just a little bit more
complex than S3, but has

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.380
almost all of the features,
all of the payoff of S4.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.200
Now, whenever I
hear about someone

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:23.380
trying to solve a problem with
there's two existing solutions

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:26.080
by creating a third
solution, I always

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.170
think of this XKCD comic.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:33.510
And we really want to
avoid this with R7.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.510
And we don't want to make
the whole problem more

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:38.900
complicated by now giving
people three possible choices

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:42.080
of object oriented programming
instead of the two that

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.370
are baked into base R.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.790
Not to mention like the
five or six other packages

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:52.168
on CRAN that have other object
oriented programming systems.

00:13:52.168 --> 00:13:53.460
So how are we going to do that?

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.460
Well, first of all,
this is a team effort.

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:59.250
The R Consortium working group
on object oriented programming,

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:02.450
it's the mouthful, includes
representatives from R Core,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.790
from Bioconductor,
from our rOpenSci,,

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.700
from RStudio, the tidyverse,
and the general R community.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.690
This is not an effort to
solve the problems of just

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:14.030
a small group of people,
but this is an effort

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:17.690
to solve the problems faced
by a large proportion of the R

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:20.440
community.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:22.150
Next, we're trying
to take what we

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:25.610
believe to be the best
parts of S4 and keep them.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:28.220
And that's like the formal
definition of classes.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:31.510
So when you get an object of a
specific type, you know a bunch about it

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:35.560
right off the bat. That at
helper, which gives you

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.020
a useful error message if
you try to extract something

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:40.610
that does not exist.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:44.080
R7 also provides multiple
dispatch when needed,

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.810
which is really important
for errors like for example,

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:49.450
arithmetic operations
where you need

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:52.990
to choose the implementation
based on the left hand

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.900
side and the right
hand side of addition.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:58.490
And finally, we have
this validator function,

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:01.540
which allows us to apply
additional conditions to make

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.370
sure our objects are valid.

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:08.810
And we've tried to do that
while keeping R7 100%

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:11.240
backward compatible with S3.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:14.420
So if you have an
existing S3 class,

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:17.390
you can convert it
to R7 in such a way

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.550
that everyone who
uses that object,

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.640
if it's an R7 object,
gets all of these new goodies

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.715
but no existing code breaks.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.880
And one of the ways
we've done this

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:33.510
is a little weird in
terms of the spectrum

00:15:33.510 --> 00:15:36.810
of object oriented programming
systems and other environments.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.500
So one of the core
precepts of OO programming

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.640
is normally encapsulation.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:45.720
And that means like
only the person

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:51.410
who writes a class can access
the data inside that class.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:56.090
And this is, I think, a really
good idea in 99% of programming

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:59.720
languages. But it
doesn't really work in R

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.080
because the people who
use R are a data scientists,

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.540
and they want that
data inside that object

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:07.790
and they would do
whatever it takes

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:11.060
to get that object if they
want it to get that data,

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:11.860
if they want it.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:15.110
So S4 tried to
kind of protect people

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:17.150
from this. Like you
never as a user,

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.025
you're not really supposed
to use the at symbol.

00:16:19.025 --> 00:16:21.067
You're always supposed to
use some kind of accessor

00:16:21.067 --> 00:16:23.130
function, but it just
didn't really work.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.660
People want that data.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.010
And so instead,
R7 is designed around

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:31.080
like, rather than trying to fight
this very natural tendency,

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.730
R7 accepts it while
giving you the ability

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.150
to change the
internals of your class

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:41.340
if you need to with
these dynamic properties.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.430
So if you have
to, you can change

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:44.930
the internal
implementation, you can

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:48.290
deprecate fields, properties
inside your object

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:50.540
and still have existing
code work, which

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:56.310
is a really important property to
allow code to evolve over time.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:59.550
And then finally, we've
tried to dig a pit of success

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:04.770
with thoughtful function names,
argument names, documentation

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:05.920
and errors.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:08.250
So hopefully after you've
used it a little bit,

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:11.940
you can start to guess
what the code you need is,

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.730
and if that guess
is slightly wrong,

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.460
you will get useful
feedback that points you

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:18.730
in the right direction.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:24.530
So really, really,
really appreciate it

00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:26.280
if you would, try
out R7.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.600
As I said, it's currently
a GitHub package

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:31.130
which you can install
pretty easily if you want.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:33.670
We'd love to know not just
what doesn't work

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:36.290
or how it doesn't help
you solve your problems,

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:39.800
but we also want to know
what doesn't make sense.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:41.950
What don't you understand?

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:44.197
Like, where are the problems
in the documentation,

00:17:44.197 --> 00:17:46.030
where are the problems
in the error messages,

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:48.730
so that we can do
better and that we

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:51.370
can make R7 just right.

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:53.310
Thank you.

